---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How do I calculate percentage of marks?', a: 'Choose "X is what % of Y", enter marks obtained as the part and total marks as the whole. The answer is marks obtained divided by total marks, times 100.' },
  { q: 'What is the formula for percentage?', a: 'Percentage = (part ÷ whole) × 100. To find a percentage of a number, use (percentage ÷ 100) × number.' },
  { q: 'How is percentage change different from percentage points?', a: 'Percentage change compares the new value with the old one. Going from 40% to 50% is a rise of 10 percentage points but a 25% increase.' },
  { q: 'Can the answer be above 100%?', a: 'Yes. If the part is bigger than the whole, the percentage is over 100. A fall can never be more than 100%, though.' },
  { q: 'Does this save my numbers?', a: 'No. Everything is worked out in your browser as you type, and nothing is stored or sent anywhere.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout slug="percentage-calculator" faqs={faqs}>
  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: lead paragraph, drafted by AI. */}
  <p slot="lead">
    This percentage calculator works out marks percentage, a percent of a number, percentage change and increases or
    decreases. Type two numbers, pick what you want to find, and see the answer with the working shown.
  </p>

  <div slot="tool">
    <div class="field">
      <label for="pct-mode">What do you want to find?</label>
      <select id="pct-mode">
        <option value="of">What is X% of a number?</option>
        <option value="what">X is what % of Y? (marks percentage)</option>
        <option value="change">Percentage change from old to new</option>
        <option value="inc">Increase a number by X%</option>
        <option value="dec">Decrease a number by X%</option>
      </select>
    </div>
    <div class="row two">
      <div class="field"><label for="pct-a" id="pct-la">Percentage (%)</label><input id="pct-a" type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
      <div class="field"><label for="pct-b" id="pct-lb">Number</label><input id="pct-b" type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
    </div>
    <div class="result" aria-live="polite">
      <p class="label" style="margin:0">Answer</p>
      <p class="big" id="pct-out">–</p>
      <p id="pct-work" class="hint">Enter both numbers to see the answer and the working.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: how-to and worked example drafted by AI. Add a classroom example of your own. */}
  <h2>How to use the percentage calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Pick the question you want answered from the list.</li>
    <li>Enter the two numbers. The labels change to match your choice.</li>
    <li>Read the answer and the working line underneath it, which shows the formula with your numbers.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p class="worked">
    A student scores 432 out of 600 marks. Choose "X is what % of Y", enter 432 as the part and 600 as the whole. The
    working reads 432 ÷ 600 × 100 = 72%. To see the improvement from a previous 60% to 72%, choose "Percentage
    change", enter 60 and 72, and you get a 20% increase, because the gain of 12 is one fifth of 60.
  </p>
  <h2>Where percentages trip people up</h2>
  <p>
    The most common slip is dividing by the wrong number. For marks, the whole is the total marks, not the passing
    marks. For change, the base is always the old value. Rounding also matters: this tool keeps up to four decimal
    places in the working and rounds the main answer for readability. If a form or board wants a specific rounding
    rule, follow its instructions.
  </p>
  <script>import '../../scripts/percentage.js';</script>
</ToolLayout>
